From: Elena Maher <12779416+elenaOnline@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 06:41:16 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] gpui: Add gradient paints for meshes
 (Window::paint_mesh_gradient)

A mesh could be painted with any Background, whose gradient has two stops,
an angle and spans the draw's bounds on screen. A canvas filling shapes
with SVG gradients (any number of stops, a line or a focal point and a
circle placed in the shape's own space, padded, reflected or repeated) had
no way to draw them: the stops, the geometry and the space they live in
move, turn and scale with the shape.

MeshGradient describes one: its stops (straight colours, interpolated
straight, as SVG and the HTML canvas interpolate them), a line
(MeshGradientShape::Linear) or two circles (Radial: the two-point conical
gradient of canvas createRadialGradient and SVG 2), a spread (pad, reflect,
repeat) and the map from mesh space to the gradient's space.
Window::paint_mesh_gradient(mesh, transformation, gradient, paint) paints a
mesh with it: each fragment's colour is the vertex colour times the paint
times the gradient's colour there. MeshGradient::color_at is the
evaluation on the CPU, which the shaders follow: a degenerate gradient (a
line of no length, two equal circles) or one stop paints the last stop's
colour, and outside a cone between two circles nothing is painted.

The Metal renderer writes a batch's gradients and their stops to the
instance buffer (MeshGradientInstance, MeshGradientStopInstance) and a
draw refers to its gradient by index, so a batch with gradients stays one
instanced draw call. The vertex shader maps each vertex into its
gradient's space (a gradient is affine in mesh space, so the
interpolated position is exact) and the fragment shader finds the offset
there, spreads it and interpolates between the stops around it. Draws
without a gradient read nothing more.

wgpu and DirectX rasterize meshes through their path pipelines, where a
gradient mesh is drawn in the gradient's average colour
(MeshDraw::path_vertices, MeshGradient::average_color) until their
retained mesh pipelines are wired.

Tests (gpui_apple): the GPU agrees with color_at pixel for pixel on
linear and radial gradients (reflected, repeated, padded, a focal point
outside its circle, a skewed gradient space, a turned mesh, a faded
paint); color_at's interpolation, spreads, hard edges and degenerate
cases.

